Changelog: 18-11-2026 15:27:51

Added. Text to Speech, a Nexus app in dev/text/ (owner, 18-11-2026). It grew from a small Gradio example into a full
app, and Nexus Dashboard has its tile. The window speaks a text with the Kokoro model on the machine itself and saves
the speech. It has a voice list (the model's 15 English female voices, American and British, Heart by default), a
speed (0.50 to 2.00, 0.85 by default), Speak (Ctrl+Enter), Stop (Esc) and Save as (Ogg Vorbis, MP3, FLAC or WAV, 24 kHz
mono).

What it does better than the example:

- The text is spoken a sentence at a time, and played while the rest is made. On the test machine the first sound
  comes after 0.7 s. The example played nothing until all of it was made.
- The engine is a process that stays, so the model loads once and the window never waits on it.
- Stop and a new Speak take effect at the next sentence.
- A text that was just spoken is saved in any format without making it again. An unspoken text is made without
  playing.
- The half-precision model (164 MB) takes the place of the full one (326 MB). On a CPU both made 13 s of speech in
  4.3 s. The 8-bit model took 9.8 s.
- Each model file is downloaded to a .part file and kept only when its SHA-256 is the pinned one.
- A long speech is written a second at a time. libsndfile's Ogg writer crashed on two minutes handed over in one
  piece.
- An abbreviation, an initial and a lower-case word end no sentence. An empty line is a longer pause.

English female voices only (owner, 18-11-2026). The model's male voices and its voices in other languages are not
offered, and the engine refuses them. A text with letters of another script is not spoken, and the status line says
so. Another language in Latin letters cannot be told from English, and is read with English sounds.

The speech engine (textspeech/engine.py) has a venv of its own in ~/.local/share/nexus-text-to-speech/, beside its
model files. The reason is that onnxruntime has no wheel for the dev venv's Python 3.15. The window's Set up the
speech engine makes that venv with uv, or with a Python from 3.10 to 3.13 on the PATH. It then installs
engine-requirements.txt and fetches the model, and shows each step's output. The app runs from the checkout with the
dev venv, takes Nexus's design and icon, and is never released (.checkout-only). install.sh writes the launcher, the
menu entry and the Desktop icon for this checkout. install.sh --uninstall --purge removes the speech engine and the
settings.
